From 234271efe0ba9510fc9d732dd499dffe2e68a040 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Marius Gehrmann <marius@gehrmann.solutions>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 21:12:39 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 01/12] pmdomain: apple: pmp-report: Request T8122 devices by
 soc-device record

The T8122 PMP firmware reads the PTD request word (SOC-DEV-PS-REQ) as one
bit per record of its apple,tunable-soc-device table, in table order: bit
N is record N. The report driver assumes that bit N names soc-device ID
N + 1. That is the same thing on T6030, whose records are in ID order,
but not on T8122. On J613 the table is ordered DCS, AMCC, IOA0, IOA1,
SOC-AON, SOC-REST, EACC0, PACC0, AGX, ANE0, ISP, DISP, DISPEXT, ...,
SCODEC, LTR, ANS, so DISP (ID 8) is record 11, DISPEXT (ID 9) record 12
and ANS (ID 18) record 19.

The seeded request 0x20080 (bits 7 and 17) therefore asks the PMP for
PACC0 and SCODEC. The running firmware's own data shows exactly that: its
active set is its always-on set plus the request bits taken as records.
DISP is never active; its acknowledgment on bit 7 only appears because
PACC0 is always on. With DISP inactive the PMP ignores the internal DCP's
DISP_INT DVFS votes. The panel only needs the lowest state, so nothing
shows, but a mode on the internal pipe that needs a higher state would
wait for it forever. DISPEXT, which the external display processor needs,
has the same problem and does wait forever: its firmware polls DVFS-STATE
in its first modeset for a DISP_EXT state that the PMP never grants while
DISPEXT is inactive.

Let a report entry say that it is requested by soc-device record
(apple,soc-device-record). Its reg stays the soc-device ID - 1, so entries
keep their names and lookups by reg keep working; the driver looks up the
record that holds that ID in the PMP's table and uses its index as the
PTD request and acknowledge bit. An ID that is missing or appears twice,
or a bit that two entries would share, refuses the PMP start. Record bits
are checked against their record's acknowledge flag, the others by ID as
before. Looking the ID up keeps this right on another T8122 board whose
table is ordered differently.

Mark the T8122 DISP entry, so DISP is requested on bit 11 on J613. The
ANS entry stays on bit 17 (record 17, SCODEC on J613) for now: ANS is not
acknowledged, and asking for ANS instead of SCODEC changes what the PMP
does for storage, which has not been tested.

An equivalent opt-in version was tested on J613 (MacBook Air M3, 14.7
firmware): request 0x21800 (DISP and DISPEXT by record, ANS unchanged),
acknowledged 0x218f0; panel, GPU and the external display work.

